        { "$comment": "★★ P42 lane S (same residual, and the second consumer of D-CSUBSET-APPLIESTO-CANNOT-EXPRESS-FUNCTION-POINTER-OBJECT's fifth spelling): the FUNCTION-OR-FUNCTION-POINTER half of the inert vocabulary. Split from the row above BY MEASUREMENT rather than by category — clang 18.1.3 and gcc 13.3.0 are both SILENT on `__attribute__((format(printf,1,2))) int (*fp)(const char*,...)` and on the `nonnull` / `sentinel` / `access` / `nothrow` twins, while both DIAGNOSE `cold`, `hot`, `leaf`, `malloc` and `alloc_size` in that same position. clang says so in its own message text for the data-object case: `'nothrow' attribute only applies to functions and function pointers`.\n★★ THIS ROW IS THE REASON THE CHEAP FIX IS REFUTED. Mapping a `Ptr<FnSig>` variable to `function` at the gate — the shortcut the registry row's option (a) invited — would have silenced the row ABOVE, which both references diagnose on a function pointer. `functionPointer` had to be a fifth spelling in the `appliesTo` vocabulary, additive to `variable`, so the two halves can disagree. That is a fact that VARIES PER ROW, which is what makes it config rather than an engine rule.\n★ `noreturn` sits here rather than in the function-only row for a reason that is DSS's own behaviour, not a reference measurement: TF-C94's `noreturnCarrier` already HONORS `noreturn` on a `Ptr<FnSig>` declarator (witnessed by Tcl 9's three `TclStubs` members), and clang honors it there too. Warning on a position where a shipped sink consumes the fact would contradict the compiler's own behaviour — the worst kind of diagnostic.\n★ `access` is judged on gcc alone and that is deliberate: clang does not know the attribute at all (`unknown attribute 'access' ignored`), so its diagnosis carries no applicability signal. gcc's does: silent on a function and on a function pointer, `'access' attribute only applies to function types` on a data object and on a typedef.", "$p69NoreturnKeywordNameComment": "P69 round 4 (lane cs): `_Noreturn` WRITTEN AS AN ATTRIBUTE NAME joins this row — `[[_Noreturn]] void k(void);`, which C23 6.7.13.7 makes the same attribute as `[[noreturn]]`. MEASURED before it joined: this compiler HONOURED the spelling (its noreturn scan reads the keyword token wherever it is written) and ALSO warned `S_UnknownAttribute: _Noreturn` — it called unknown a name it was acting on. gcc 13.3.0 and mingw-w64 gcc 13.2.0 accept it in silence; clang 18.1.3 and Apple clang accept it and note that the '[[_Noreturn]]' spelling is deprecated in C23; cl refuses it (C3750). Silent here, as gcc is: a deprecation note is advice about style, and this compiler is never louder than gcc.", "names": ["noreturn", "format", "nonnull", "nothrow", "access", "sentinel"], "appliesTo": ["function", "functionPointer"], "effect": "none" },
